    <body>
      <sec id="article-169724.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Sleep plays a critical role in brain maturation. In the developing brain, sleep impairment profoundly alters the regulation of monoamines, acetylcholine, and neuropeptides, disrupts toxin clearance through the glymphatic system,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;and interferes with hormonal balance and inflammatory processes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Sleep-wake disorders include insomnia, obstructive sleep apnea, parasomnias, narcolepsy, and restless leg syndrome. Narcolepsy represents a chronic condition characterized by severe excessive daytime sleepiness (EDS). The ICSD-3 classifies narcolepsy into 2 types: type 1, which includes cataplexy, and type 2, which lacks cataplexy. Both types present with persistent fatigue and impaired ability to maintain wakefulness during the day. REM sleep characteristics aid in distinguishing narcolepsy from other central hypersomnolence disorders and in differentiating type 1 from type 2.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Rapid eye movement (REM) sleep, associated with activation in the thalamus and amygdala, typically begins within the first 90 minutes after sleep onset and recurs in 90- to 120-minute cycles throughout the night, resulting in 3 to 5 REM periods per night. In children with narcolepsy, REM latency markedly shortens, with entry occurring within 8 minutes of a nap and often repeating multiple times during the day.&#x000a0;Approximately two-thirds of cases involve cataplexy, defining type 1 narcolepsy. Cataplexy presents as sudden generalized muscle paralysis triggered by strong emotions, eg, laughter or anger. Cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin-1 (orexin-A) levels below 110 pg/mL occur in nearly all patients with cataplexy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r4">[4]</xref> In contrast, type 2 narcolepsy lacks cataplexy and demonstrates normal hypocretin-1 levels, suggesting a distinct underlying mechanism for EDS.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s2" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Type 1 narcolepsy results from the destruction of a specific cluster of hypocretin-1 (orexin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus responsible for synthesizing the wake-promoting neuropeptide hypocretin-1.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r5">[5]</xref> Associations with environmental triggers, including H1N1 influenza, streptococcal infections, and H1N1 vaccination with the AS03 adjuvant (Pandemrix&#x000ae;), suggest immune-mediated mechanisms involving activation of T and B cells. These findings support an autoimmune process linked to human leukocyte antigen HLA-DR.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r6">[6]</xref> Observed seasonal patterns further reinforce the influence of early-life environmental exposures on disease development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Most patients with narcolepsy type 1 and half of patients with narcolepsy type 2 carry HLA-DQB1*06:02. The genetic susceptibility of narcolepsy can result from the heterodimer formed by HLA-DQA1*01:02 and HLA-DQB1*06:02, which presents cataplexy in all ethnic populations via molecular mimicry against hypocretin-1 neurons to the T cell receptor (TCR).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Less is known about the cause of narcolepsy type 2 (narcolepsy without cataplexy), but it&#x000a0;may be related to a less severe loss of these same neurons.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s3" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Pediatric narcolepsy is a rare disease, with a global average prevalence of between 20 and 50 per 100,000; the prevalence is 37.7/100,000 individuals in the United States.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;The reported prevalence of narcolepsy varies in studies from different countries and with different ethnicities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;The lowest prevalence is reported in Israel (0.23 per 100,000), while the highest is in Japan (160 per 100,000). Although some studies suggest a higher prevalence in males, narcolepsy affects males and females equally.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The prevalence of narcolepsy type 1 has been observed to rise following influenza A (H1N1) pdm09 and the administration of the Pandemrix influenza vaccine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s4" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The pathogenesis of narcolepsy remains inadequately elucidated.&#x000a0;The association between narcolepsy and the&#x000a0;HLA-DQB1*06:02&#x000a0;allele (which increases the risk of narcolepsy by 200%) indicates an autoimmune etiology for this condition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;The HLA-bias and increased frequencies of autoreactive&#x000a0;CD4<sup>+</sup>&#x000a0;T cells against hypocretin in peripheral blood suggest autoimmune processes in the hypothalamus. Also,&#x000a0;the most strongly upregulated genes in narcolepsy patients have been shown to code for MTRNR2L12 and MTRNR2L8 peptides, which are homologous to the mitochondria-encoded HUMANIN peptide, known to play a role in other neurological diseases, including Alzheimer's disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Specific T cell subsets, including CD4<sup>+</sup>&#x000a0;and CD8<sup>+</sup>&#x000a0;T cells, target and destroy hypocretin-1 neurons.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s5" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Symptoms of narcolepsy typically begin in childhood, as young as&#x000a0;6 years of age or older. More severe symptoms correlate with type 1 narcolepsy.&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;hallmark of narcolepsy is excessive daytime sleepiness, described by children as mental cloudiness or "fog", especially during inactivity,&#x000a0;such as watching TV, riding in a car, sitting in a classroom, or doing homework. The tetrad associated with narcolepsy consists of excessive daytime sleepiness (EDS), cataplexy, sleep paralysis, and hypnagogic hallucinations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Children with narcolepsy, however, can be mistaken for depression or attention deficit disorder, the former coexisting&#x000a0;in a quarter of the patients, and the latter in 30%. Interestingly, psychopathology precedes the diagnosis of narcolepsy in most patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;At the time of narcolepsy diagnosis, children are frequently obese (in 25%&#x02013;50% of children) due to nocturnal eating, have parasomnia, somnambulism, and ADHD more than adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>A constant feeling of malaise and fatigue is typically reported.&#x000a0;Symptoms might appear gradually over the course of the disease, though not every child experiences all of them. Furthermore, precocious puberty related to hypocretin has also been noted.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Other common symptoms in children include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Sleep attacks:</bold>&#x000a0;Involuntary, overwhelming urges to sleep that can happen anywhere, anytime, and last about 15 to 30 minutes, usually longer in children than adults.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cataplexy:</bold>&#x000a0;Sudden, brief (seconds to minutes) descending muscle paralysis (usually affects the face and neck first before spreading into the limbs)&#x000a0;without loss of consciousness, triggered by strong emotions like laughter, anger, or frustration; the symptoms could range from mild ptosis, tongue protrusion, dysarthria or facial droop (slack-jawed) to dropping items from loss of grip, and spells of knees buckling and collapsing to the ground for a few minutes, which occurs in approximately 60% to 70% of people with narcolepsy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Sleep paralysis:</bold>&#x000a0;Characterized by the inability to move or speak when falling asleep or waking up, often with hallucinations, usually lasting seconds to minutes.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hypnagogic/hypnopompic hallucinations:</bold>&#x000a0;Characterized by sleep terrors, often frightening, vivid visual or auditory hallucinations at the onset of sleep or upon awakening.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Fragmented nighttime sleep:</bold>&#x000a0;Patients with fragmented sleep wake up frequently during the night; restless limb syndrome and somnambulance may be present.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Automatic/behavioral symptoms:</bold>&#x000a0;Patients may fall asleep but continue their ongoing activities, eg, writing; attention deficit, hyperactivity, social withdrawal, anxiety, or depression; irritability,&#x000a0;aggressiveness, or memory deficit.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s6" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Screening tools and&#x000a0;questionnaires, eg, the Idiopathic Hypersomnia Severity Scale, Epworth Sleepiness Scale-Child Adolescent (ESS-CHAD), and the Pediatric Daytime Sleepiness Scale (PDSS), could help collect information on&#x000a0;sleepiness and treatment responses. The modified Cataplexy Diary&#x000a0;uses&#x000a0;child-friendly terminology. The ESS-CHAD comes in&#x000a0;2 versions: one with a 1-month recall period for general use, and the other for research, with a recall period of &#x0201c;since your last study visit&#x0201d;.</p>
        <p>During the office evaluation, the parents could be asked if their child does any of the following:</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p>Fight to stay awake throughout the day?</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Feel a constant need to sleep?</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Take long daytime naps, even if they are past the age of typical daytime napping?</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Feel likely to doze off in less active environments?</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fall back to sleep after being awakened in the morning?</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The next appropriate step would be to obtain a polysomnography (PSG) concurrently with a daytime&#x000a0;multiple sleep latency test&#x000a0;(MSLT). PSG and MSLT should be preceded by at least 1 week of actigraphic recording or a sleep log.&#x000a0;Actigraphy measures movement and collects data via a wrist-watch-type device that can be worn for up to 2 weeks, in conjunction with a sleep log to document sleep and wake times. Additionally, a blood test for the HLA DQB1*06:02 haplotype and CSF hypocretin levels is available if the index of suspicion for narcolepsy is high, despite equivalent MSLT results.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s7" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Management Approach</bold>
</p>
        <p>Narcolepsy has no cure, but symptoms can be managed with lifestyle modifications and medications, including the following strategies:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Medications:</bold>&#x000a0;Prescribed to control sleepiness and cataplexy. Psychostimulants methylphenidate and amphetamines for EDS, and sodium oxybate for EDS and cataplexy. Modafinil for EDS in patients without cataplexy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r26">[26]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lifestyle:</bold>&#x000a0;Strict sleep schedules, regular short naps, exercise, and creating an Individualized Health Plan (IHP) with schools, which may include:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Taking&#x000a0;1 or 2 naps for&#x000a0;15 to 20 minutes during the day; naps at mid-morning and after lunch may be especially helpful</p></list-item><list-item><p>Taking frequent breaks from sitting or other sedentary activities to take short walks</p></list-item><list-item><p>Avoiding heavy meals and medications that cause sleepiness</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Education:</bold>&#x000a0;Informing family, friends, and teachers helps them understand the condition and provide support.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Additionally Pharmacologic Strategies</bold>
</p>
        <p>WAKIX<sup>&#x000ae;</sup> (pitolisant), a piperidine hydrochloride derivative, received FDA approval in 2019.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r27">[27]</xref> This agent functions as a histamine-3 receptor antagonist and inverse agonist and carries an indication for the treatment of EDS in pediatric patients aged 6 years and older with narcolepsy. However, the exact mechanism of action remains incompletely defined. Dosing follows a weekly escalating titration beginning at 4.45 mg/day and increasing to 17.8 mg/day. In children weighing 40 kg or more, the maximum recommended dose reaches 35.6 mg once daily. Careful use remains essential in patients with moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh class B), renal impairment (eGFR &#x0003c;60 mL/minute/1.73 m&#x000b2;), and those identified as poor CYP2D6 metabolizers. Baseline electrocardiogram assessment for QT interval prolongation supports safe initiation of therapy.</p>
        <p>During the placebo-controlled phase of clinical studies, the most common adverse reactions occurring in &#x02265;5% of patients and at rates exceeding placebo included headache (19%) and insomnia (7%).&#x000a0;Oveporexton (TAK-861), an oral orexin receptor 2-selective agonist, is an alternative therapeutic option and has demonstrated improvements in wakefulness, reductions in sleepiness, and decreased cataplexy over an 8-week treatment period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r28">[28]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s8" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Cataplexy and automatic behavioral symptoms can be misconstrued as ADHD, seizures, or attention-seeking behavior in young children. Brain tumor-associated narcolepsy has also been reported, involving the hypothalamus itself.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r29">[29]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s9" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of pediatric narcolepsy is characterized by a chronic, lifelong course without cure, though symptoms are generally nonprogressive and can be effectively managed with appropriate treatment. Early-onset disease, often beginning in childhood, may be associated with more severe manifestations, particularly in type 1 narcolepsy due to hypocretin deficiency. With timely diagnosis and a combination of pharmacologic therapy, behavioral strategies, and educational support, many patients achieve improved symptom control and functional outcomes. Further research is needed to evaluate the outcomes of investigational therapies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>However, delays in diagnosis, common due to symptom overlap with psychiatric or developmental conditions, can worsen academic performance, psychosocial development, and quality of life. Comorbid conditions, eg, obesity, metabolic disturbances, and mood disorders, may further influence long-term outcomes. Lifelong management, regular monitoring, and individualized care plans remain essential for optimizing daily functioning and supporting normal developmental trajectories.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r31">[31]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Pediatric narcolepsy carries significant complications that extend beyond sleep disruption, affecting neurologic, cognitive, and psychosocial development. Core symptoms, excessive daytime sleepiness, fragmented sleep, cataplexy, sleep paralysis, and hypnagogic hallucinations, are often present atypically in children, with prolonged nighttime sleep, frequent daytime naps, hyperactivity, and irritability. These features commonly mimic attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, depression, or parasomnias, leading to delayed or missed diagnosis. Untreated disease contributes to impaired academic performance, memory deficits, emotional dysregulation, and social withdrawal. Cataplexy in children may present subtly, further complicating recognition. Additionally, comorbidities, eg, obesity, metabolic disturbances, and mood disorders, increase overall disease burden and interfere with normal growth and development.</p>
        <p>Long-term complications arise from the chronic nature of narcolepsy and limitations in evidence-based pediatric treatments. Many therapies are used off-label, and data on safety and efficacy across developmental stages remain limited. Persistent symptoms negatively impact quality of life, family dynamics, and peer relationships. Inadequate symptom control may also increase the risk of injury due to sleep attacks or sudden muscle weakness. Early, accurate diagnosis and comprehensive management, including behavioral strategies, pharmacotherapy, and treatment of comorbid conditions, remain critical to minimizing complications and supporting optimal developmental, academic, and psychosocial outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-169724.r31">[31]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s11" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>A team of specialists experienced in diagnosing and treating narcolepsy in children and adolescents constitutes a panel of pediatric neurology, developmental medicine, psychology, and pulmonology.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s12" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Although diagnostic criteria in ICSD-3 outlined the common symptoms and signs of narcolepsy, several differences in presentation have been noted between adult and pediatric narcolepsy patients. In addition, the symptoms of each narcoleptic patient can change between different life stages. For example, some children who develop cataplexy later may be diagnosed as having narcolepsy without cataplexy initially. The differences between children and the adult population may be crucial in diagnosis and tailoring treatment strategies for pediatric narcolepsy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Narcolepsy is a chronic sleep-wake disorder with significant clinical implications in pediatric populations, where early onset can impair neurodevelopment, cognition, and psychosocial functioning. Pathophysiology in type 1 narcolepsy involves autoimmune-mediated destruction of hypocretin-1&#x02013;producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, often associated with HLA-DQB1*06:02 and environmental triggers. Type 2 narcolepsy lacks cataplexy and demonstrates less clearly defined mechanisms. Clinical presentation includes excessive daytime sleepiness, cataplexy, sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations, fragmented sleep, and behavioral changes such as irritability or hyperactivity, frequently leading to misdiagnosis. Evaluation requires careful clinical assessment supported by polysomnography and multiple sleep latency testing, with adjunctive use of actigraphy, CSF hypocretin levels, and HLA testing in select cases. Management focuses on symptom control through pharmacologic therapy, including stimulants, sodium oxybate, and histaminergic agents, alongside structured sleep routines and behavioral interventions.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration plays a critical role in improving patient-centered outcomes and reducing diagnostic delays and complications. Physicians and sleep specialists lead diagnostic confirmation and treatment planning, while primary care clinicians and advanced practitioners facilitate early recognition, longitudinal monitoring, and timely referral. Nurses support patient and family education, reinforce adherence, and monitor symptom progression and safety risks such as sleep attacks. Pharmacists optimize medication selection, dosing, and monitoring for adverse effects and drug interactions. Mental health professionals address comorbid psychiatric conditions, and school personnel contribute to individualized education plans that accommodate daytime sleep needs. Coordinated communication and shared decision-making across disciplines enhance treatment adherence, minimize risks, and support long-term developmental, academic, and psychosocial outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-169724.s14">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
